 "location": {
  "name": "Banff National Park",
  "wiki": "Banff_National_Park",
  "country": "Canada",
  "region": "Alberta, Canadian Rockies",
  "lat": 51.5,
  "lon": -116.0,
  "tagline": "Canada's first national park: glacier-fed turquoise lakes under a wall of Rocky Mountain peaks.",
  "intro": "Banff National Park covers 6,641 km² of the Canadian Rockies about 128 km west of Calgary. It began in 1885 as a small reserve around a set of hot springs and grew into a park of icefields, glacial lakes like Lake Louise and Moraine Lake, and the resort town of Banff. It is part of the Canadian Rocky Mountain Parks UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
  "stats": [
   {
    "label": "Established",
    "value": "1885"
   },
   {
    "label": "Area",
    "value": "6,641 km² (2,564 sq mi)"
   },
   {
    "label": "Visitors",
    "value": "≈4.56 million (2025–26)"
   },
   {
    "label": "UNESCO World Heritage",
    "value": "Since 1984"
   }
  ],
  "facts": [
   "The park exists because of a dispute over hot springs: railway workers found the Cave and Basin springs in 1883, and in 1885 Prime Minister John A. Macdonald set aside 26 km² around them for the public.",
   "Originally called Rocky Mountains Park, it was Canada's first national park and only the third in North America, after Yellowstone and Mackinac.",
   "The Icefields Parkway toward Jasper crosses Bow Summit at 2,088 m (6,850 ft), the highest point on any public road in Canada.",
   "Six wildlife overpasses plus underpasses cross the Trans-Canada Highway; with fencing along 82 km, they have cut wildlife–vehicle collisions by more than 80%.",
